k the Seven Pairs pattern (must be all concealed pairs).  \n\nSo best to go for standard hand.  \n\nNow, which tile to discard? After forming B3-B4-B5 chow, I have left: B2, B2, B7, B8, B8. I have two pairs (B2 and B8) and a single B7. I need one more meld. Options:  \n- Discard B7: then I have two pairs, need one more meld (could be pung of B2 or B8, or a chow using B7? but B7 discarded).  \n- Discard one of the pairs? If I discard a B2 or B8, I break a pair, leaving me with a single and a pair, still need two melds. That's worse.  \n\nSo discard B7 seems best, because B7 is isolated and only 2 left, and to form a chow with B8 I need B9, which is possible but less likely than drawing a B2 or B8 for pung (since B2 has 4 left, B8 has 2 left). Also, B7 is a terminal? B7 is not terminal (terminal are 1 and 9). So no issue.  \n\nBut wait, if I discard B7, I lose the chance for B7-B8-B9 chow. But I have B8 pair, so if I draw B9, I can use one B8 and B7? But B7 discarded, so no. So I would need to draw B9 and then use B8 pair to make chow? Actually, if I have B8 pair and draw B9, I have B8,B8,B9 \u2014 not a chow. I would need B7 also. So discarding B7 kills the chow possibility. But maybe that's okay because the chow chance is low (need B9, and then I would have to break B8 pair, leaving me with single B8).  \n\nAlternatively, discard B5? But B5 is part of a chow. If I discard B5, I break the chow, leaving B3,B4 which are not a meld. Then I have B2 pair, B3, B4, B7, B8 pair. That's messier. So definitely keep the chow.  \n\nDiscard B7 seems the best.  \n\nBut let's check other players' melds: Previous player has B1 kong, B6-B7-B8 chow, B5 pung. So many B tiles are used. B5 is already pung by previous player, so only 1 B5 left (as per count). B6 is used in chow, so 0 left. B7 is used in chow, so 2 left. B8 is used in chow? B6-B7-B8 uses one B8, so B8 count 2 left (I have 2, so all remaining?). Actually unshown B8:2, I have 2, so all B8 are in my hand. That means no one else can have B8, so I can only get B8 from drawing the last two? But I already have both, so no more B8 to draw. So B8 pair cannot become pung because no more B8 available. So I should discard one B8? Because keeping a pair that cannot become pung is okay for pair, but if I need a pung, it's impossible. But I can use B8 pair as the final pair. So I need one more meld, which could be B2 pung (4 left) or a chow with B7 and something. But if I discard B7, then my only hope for a meld is B2 pung. B2 has 4 left, so good chance.  \n\nIf I discard B8, I break the pair, leaving single B8, and I have B7, then maybe chow with B8 and B9? But need B9. B9 has 3 left. That's possible. But then I would have B2 pair, B3-B4-B5 chow, B7, B8, and need two more tiles to complete hand (one meld and pair? Actually, after discarding one B8, I have 5 tiles: B2 pair, B3,B4,B5 (chow), B7, B8. That's 5 tiles, which is one meld (chow) and two singles. I need another meld and a pair. So I would need to get a pair from one of the singles and a meld from the other. Complicated.  \n\nBetter to keep B8 pair as potential final pair. So discard B7.  \n\nThus, the most promising move is Play B7.  \n\nReason: Forming the chow B3-B4-B5 maximizes meld potential, and discarding the isolated B7 preserves pairs and focuses on drawing B2 for a pung or possibly a chow with B8-B9 if I draw B9 later, but primary plan is to get B2 pung. B7 is the least connected tile given current hand and tile availability.  \n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
